\section{VALIDATION}

To demonstrate the effectiveness of the geometric
correction framework developed in the preceding sections, we present a simple numerical example illustrating
stability deviation, error displacement, and sovereignty
restoration.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026,DuttonSEFI2026}
The purpose of this validation is not to
model a specific photonic device, but to show how geometric worldline analysis provides a clear and reproducible mechanism for identifying and correcting photonic errors. In the DEFI-centric interpretation, this example illustrates how worldlines depart from and are restored to the DEFI stability manifold. Figure~\ref{fig:validation_surface}
illustrates the saddle-shaped stability surface used in this example.

Consider a stability surface $\Sigma$ embedded in a threedimensional configuration space $M$. For validation, we
choose a saddle-shaped surface defined by


\[
\Sigma = \{(x, y, z) \in \mathbb{R}^{3} \mid z = \alpha x^{2} - \beta y^{2}\},
\]


where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are positive constants.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026}
This surface exhibits both convergent and divergent curvature directions, providing a nontrivial geometric structure for testing stability and correction. The geometric invariants
of ideal evolution are determined by the curvature and
torsion of worldlines constrained to $\Sigma$.

An ideal worldline $\gamma_{\Sigma}(t)$ on the stability surface may
be parameterized as


\[
\gamma_{\Sigma}(t) = (t, \sin t, \alpha t^{2} - \beta \sin^{2} t),
\]


which satisfies the sovereignty conditions associated with
$\Sigma$.\cite{DuttonSEFI2026} The curvature and torsion of this worldline, denoted
$\kappa_{\Sigma}(t)$ and $\tau_{\Sigma}(t)$, serve as the geometric invariants of ideal
evolution. In the DEFI interpretation, these invariants
represent identity-preserving dynamical constraints.

To simulate an error, we introduce a displaced worldline


\[
\gamma(t) = \gamma_{\Sigma}(t) + \epsilon v(t),
\]


where $\epsilon$ is a small perturbation parameter and $v(t)$ is a
deviation vector chosen to represent a physically meaningful photonic error.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026}
For validation, we select


\[
v(t) = (0, 0, \cos t),
\]


corresponding to a binormal displacement that models
spectral or phase drift. Figure~\ref{fig:validation_displacement}
illustrates the displaced worldline oscillating above and below the stability surface.

The displaced worldline no longer
satisfies the sovereignty conditions, and its curvature and
torsion deviate from the geometric invariants of ideal evolution.

The displacement vector is computed as


\[
d(t) = \gamma(t) - \Pi_{\Sigma}(\gamma(t)),
\]


where $\Pi_{\Sigma}$ denotes the geometric projection onto the stability surface.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026}
For the saddle surface defined above, the
projection is given by


\[
\Pi_{\Sigma}(x, y, z) = (x, y, \alpha x^{2} - \beta y^{2}).
\]


The magnitude of the displacement vector,


\[
\|d(t)\| = |z - (\alpha x^{2} - \beta y^{2})|,
\]


quantifies the severity of the error.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026}
In this example, the
displacement magnitude oscillates with $\cos t$, reflecting
the periodic nature of the simulated spectral drift.

Correction is performed by applying the geometric projection,


\[
\gamma_{\mathrm{corr}}(t) = \Pi_{\Sigma}(\gamma(t)),
\]


which removes the displacement vector and restores the
worldline to the stability surface.\cite{DuttonSEFI2026}
After correction, the
curvature and torsion of the worldline satisfy


\[
\kappa_{\mathrm{corr}}(t) = \kappa_{\Sigma}(t), \qquad \tau_{\mathrm{corr}}(t) = \tau_{\Sigma}(t),
\]


demonstrating sovereignty restoration. Figure~\ref{fig:validation_correction}
shows the corrected worldline lying precisely on the stability surface.

In the DEFI-centric interpretation, this correction corresponds to restoring the worldline to the DEFI stability manifold.\cite{DuttonSEFI2026}
The identity-layer invariants (SEFI),
geometry-layer invariants (GWFM), and dynamical invariants (DEFI) are all realigned, demonstrating unity
restoration.

This validation example confirms that the
SEFI/GWFM/DEFI geometric framework provides
a clear and reproducible method for identifying and
correcting photonic errors.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026}
The geometric representation of stability deviation, error displacement, and
sovereignty restoration forms a robust foundation
for photonic quantum error correction across diverse
physical platforms.
\section{DISCUSSION}

The geometric framework developed in this work provides a physically motivated foundation for photonic
quantum error correction.\cite{DuttonSEFI2026,DuttonElectronField2026}
By representing photonic
modes as worldlines constrained to stability surfaces, the
SEFI/GWFM/DEFI approach offers a unified description of stability, error formation, and correction that is
independent of specific physical mechanisms. This geometric perspective complements existing bosonic codes
and suggests new pathways for stabilizing quantum information in photonic systems. Figure~\ref{fig:discussion_geometry}
illustrates the unified geometric structure underlying SEFI, GWFM, and DEFI.

One of the central advantages of the geometric approach is its ability to describe photonic errors in terms
of displacement vectors and curvature/torsion deviations.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026}
Traditional QEC frameworks typically classify errors algebraically, relying on operator-based descriptions
or phase-space symmetries. In contrast, the geometric
framework interprets errors as deviations from physically
meaningful stability surfaces. This interpretation provides intuitive insight into how errors arise and how they
can be corrected. For example, amplitude damping corresponds to inward normal displacement, while spectral
drift corresponds to binormal deviation. These geometric
signatures offer a natural classification of photonic errors
that is directly tied to the physical structure of the system.

The correction mapping developed in Section 5 demonstrates how geometric projection can restore sovereignty
by returning displaced worldlines to the stability surface.\cite{DuttonSEFI2026}
This projection-based correction mechanism is compatible with a wide range of photonic architectures, including waveguides, cavities, fibers, and integrated photonic
circuits. Because the correction mapping acts on geometric invariants rather than specific physical parameters, it
provides a flexible and platform-independent method for
stabilizing photonic modes. Figure~\ref{fig:projection_mapping}
illustrates this geometric correction mechanism.

The validation example presented in Section 7 illustrates the effectiveness of geometric projection in restoring sovereignty-preserving evolution.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026}
The saddle-shaped
stability surface used in the example demonstrates how
geometric features such as curvature and torsion influence error formation and correction. The displaced worldline oscillates above and below the stability surface due
to binormal perturbation, while the corrected worldline
returns precisely to the surface, demonstrating the effectiveness of geometric projection as a correction mechanism. Figure~\ref{fig:validation_correction} shows the corrected worldline
lying on the stability surface.

The geometric QEC framework developed here suggests several avenues for future research. First, stability
surfaces may be engineered to enhance robustness against
specific types of photonic errors. For example, surfaces
with gentle curvature may provide greater tolerance to
spectral drift, while surfaces with strong curvature may
enhance confinement against amplitude damping. Second, geometric filtering techniques may be developed to
remove displacement components selectively, enabling
targeted correction of specific error types. Third, the
SEFI/GWFM/DEFI framework may be extended to
multi-mode photonic systems, where stability surfaces
become higher-dimensional manifolds and worldlines
become multi-component geometric objects.

Finally, the geometric perspective may provide new insight into the relationship between photonic QEC and
other geometric frameworks in physics.\cite{DuttonElectronField2026}
For example, the
interpretation of photonic modes as worldlines on stability surfaces parallels geometric approaches in classical
mechanics, general relativity, and field theory. The unified SEFI/GWFM/DEFI framework may therefore serve
as a bridge between photonic QEC and broader geometric theories of physical systems.

In summary, the geometric QEC framework developed
in this work provides a physically motivated, platformindependent method for stabilizing photonic quantum
information. By interpreting photonic modes as worldlines constrained to stability surfaces and errors as geometric displacements, the SEFI/GWFM/DEFI approach
offers a unified description of stability, error formation,
and correction. This geometric perspective complements
existing bosonic codes and suggests new pathways for
stabilizing quantum information in photonic systems.
